p9share -- share directories between machines over 9P, through ssh

On the machine that has the files, as yourself:

    p9share export /srv/projects bob.example.org

On the machine that should show them, once:

    sudo p9share mount alice projects /mnt/projects

That is all.  The machine with the files dials out to the other with ssh, so the
machine that shows them needs no open port and no server for you to configure.  A
daemon that starts at boot keeps the tunnel up (starting it again when it drops) and
keeps the mount right: mounted while the tunnel is there, unmounted when it is not.
The user on the receiving machine switches it without sudo:

    p9share ro projects        read-only
    p9share rw projects        read-write
    p9share off projects       not mounted

    p9share status             what is shared, what is mounted, what is running

Several directories, several machines, and both directions at once are each one more
block of configuration.  Nothing happens on a machine until something is configured on it.

The configuration is two small files in obconf, the notation of the OpenBSD daemons'
config files (p9share.conf(5)); the commands above write it, and you may edit it by hand:

    export "projects" {             # ~/.config/p9share/p9share.conf, where the files are
        directory /srv/projects
        host bob.example.org
    }

    mount "projects" {              # /etc/p9share/p9share.conf, where they are shown
        user alice
        at /mnt/projects
    }

How it works: p9share runs daud, a 9P2000.L file server (a fork of diod, in this
package), as you, unprivileged, on a private unix socket, and ssh -R hands that socket
to the other machine, into the user's ~/.p9share/.  The root daemon there mounts it with
the kernel's 9p client.

Requirements: Linux with the 9p module (Debian, Ubuntu, WSL2 and others), openssh-client,
and key-based ssh from the machine with the files to the one that shows them.
To build from source: a C compiler, autoconf, automake and autoconf-archive.

Security: the export is the directory you name, with your rights, and the mount is
nosuid,nodev.  The socket on the receiving machine belongs to that machine's user and is
mounted only if it answers; any local user there who can reach the mount point sees the
files as you.  Do not export a directory to a machine whose users you do not trust.

On WSL2 there is no systemd: sudo p9share wsl-boot puts the daemon in /etc/wsl.conf.

See INSTALL, HACKING, p9share(8), p9share.conf(5), daud(8).
License: GPL-2.0-or-later for p9share; daud is diod's licence (GPL-2, with libnpfs MIT-like), see COPYING and daud/COPYING.
